#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135116 :tutorial/content "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp$$\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" :step/math-academy-id 33903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "47945f1d-ab72-5fe8-bc2c-43a69480b323" :question/math-academy-id "e-9090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050733}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" :step/math-academy-id 33904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-9099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380593}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" :step/math-academy-id 33918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133710 :question/id #uuid "bd1f48b4-8bfc-536d-8145-0effcf44a788" :question/math-academy-id "e-9104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049545}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397956}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"} :step/id #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" :step/math-academy-id 33937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135117 :tutorial/content "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_n [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A^T} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_m [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5454 :tutorial/title "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose"} :step/id #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" :step/math-academy-id 33950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135118 :tutorial/content "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.$$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5455 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" :step/math-academy-id 33951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135119 :tutorial/content "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5456 :tutorial/title "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0" :step/math-academy-id 33952}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134047}]}
